---
import ProjectCaseLayout from '../../components/ProjectCaseLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyTOC from '../../components/CaseStudyTOC.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/site';

const project = projects.find(
  (project) => project.slug === 'hungry-hippos'
);

if (!project) {
  throw new Error('HungryHippos project not found');
}

const toc = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'gameplay', label: 'How It Works' },
  { id: 'implementation', label: 'Implementation' },
  { id: 'challenges', label: 'Engineering Challenges' },
  { id: 'hardware', label: 'Hardware' },
  { id: 'skills', label: 'Engineering Skills' },
];
---

<ProjectCaseLayout
  title={project.title}
  description={project.description}
  number={project.number}
  year={project.year}
  status={project.status}
  tagline={project.tagline}
  image={project.image}
  tags={project.tags}
>

  <!-- SIDEBAR -->

  <div slot="sidebar">
    <CaseStudyTOC items={toc} />
  </div>


  <!-- OVERVIEW -->

  <section id="overview">

    <p class="eyebrow">overview</p>

    <p class="big-copy">
        HungryHippos is an embedded C++ game running entirely on an
        LPC1768 microcontroller, combining player input, game-state logic,
        collision detection, and sprite-based graphics on a uLCD display.
    </p>

    <p>
        The player moves a character around the screen collecting fruit
        while avoiding enemies. Rather than running on a desktop computer,
        the game logic and display control execute directly on the
        microcontroller, making the project an exercise in both software
        design and embedded hardware integration.
    </p>

<section id="gameplay">

  <p class="eyebrow">how it works</p>

  <h2>
    A continuous game loop updates input, objects, interactions,
    and the display.
  </h2>

  <p>
    During gameplay, the microcontroller repeatedly reads the current
    player input, updates the position of game objects, checks whether
    objects have interacted, updates the game state, and redraws the
    screen.
  </p>

  <div class="architecture-flow">

    <div>
      <span>01</span>
      <strong>Read Input</strong>
      <p>Determine the player's requested movement.</p>
    </div>

    <span>→</span>

    <div>
      <span>02</span>
      <strong>Update State</strong>
      <p>Move the player and update game objects.</p>
    </div>

    <span>→</span>

    <div>
      <span>03</span>
      <strong>Check Interactions</strong>
      <p>Determine whether fruit or enemies were encountered.</p>
    </div>

    <span>→</span>

    <div>
      <span>04</span>
      <strong>Render</strong>
      <p>Draw the resulting state to the uLCD.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

  <!-- IMPLEMENTATION -->

  <section id="implementation">

  <p class="eyebrow">implementation</p>

  <h2>
    Structuring game entities as reusable C++ objects.
  </h2>

  <p>
    The game used C++ classes to represent different types of objects.
    Shared behavior could be placed in parent classes while individual
    objects defined properties specific to themselves, such as their
    appearance and scoring behavior.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a fruit object stores its sprite as pixel data and
    draws that sprite at its current coordinates on the uLCD. This
    connected an object's internal state directly to what appeared
    on the physical display.
  </p>


  <div class="code-block">

    <div class="code-head">
      <span>simplified object structure</span>
      <span>C++</span>
    </div>

    <pre><code>{`class Collectible : public Fruit {
public:
    Collectible() {
        scoreValue = 1;
    }

    void draw() {
        display.blit(
            x,
            y,
            width,
            height,
            sprite
        );
    }
};`}</code></pre>

  </div>

  <p class="code-note">
    Simplified representation of the implementation structure.
  </p>


  <div class="skill-grid">

    <div>
      <span class="num">01</span>
      <strong>Inheritance</strong>
      <p>
        Shared game-object behavior could be reused rather than
        duplicated between individual objects.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span class="num">02</span>
      <strong>Sprite Data</strong>
      <p>
        Graphics were represented as arrays of pixel-color values
        stored in program memory.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span class="num">03</span>
      <strong>Object State</strong>
      <p>
        Position and object properties determined how each entity
        behaved during gameplay.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span class="num">04</span>
      <strong>Embedded Rendering</strong>
      <p>
        Object state was translated into graphics rendered directly
        to the uLCD.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

<section id="challenges">

  <p class="eyebrow">engineering challenges</p>

  <h2>
    Keeping software state and the physical display synchronized.
  </h2>

  <div class="challenge-list">

    <div>

      <span>01</span>

      <div>
        <strong>Managing multiple game objects</strong>

        <p>
          Player, fruit, and enemy objects each needed independent
          position and behavior while still participating in the same
          game loop.
        </p>

        <p class="solution">
          <b>Approach:</b> organize entities as reusable C++ objects
          with common behavior separated from object-specific properties.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>


    <div>

      <span>02</span>

      <div>
        <strong>Rendering graphics on embedded hardware</strong>

        <p>
          Updating the game state alone was not enough—the corresponding
          sprite also had to be drawn at the correct screen coordinates.
        </p>

        <p class="solution">
          <b>Approach:</b> associate rendering behavior with each object's
          current state so display updates followed software updates.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>


    <div>

      <span>03</span>

      <div>
        <strong>Coordinating continuous gameplay</strong>

        <p>
          Input, movement, interactions, and graphics all needed to be
          evaluated repeatedly without one part of the application
          becoming disconnected from another.
        </p>

        <p class="solution">
          <b>Approach:</b> structure the application around a repeated
          update cycle: input → state → interaction → render.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </div>

</section>

  <!-- HARDWARE -->

  <section id="hardware">

  <p class="eyebrow">hardware integration</p>

  <h2>
    Software executing directly on an embedded platform.
  </h2>

  <div class="hardware-grid">

    <div>
      <span>controller</span>
      <strong>mbed LPC1768</strong>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>display</span>
      <strong>uLCD-144-G2</strong>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>language</span>
      <strong>C++</strong>
    </div>

  </div>

  <p>
    The LPC1768 executed the game logic while the uLCD acted as the
    graphical interface. The project therefore required the application
    code to interact directly with external hardware rather than treating
    graphics and input as operating-system services.
  </p>

</section>


  <!-- SKILLS -->

 <section id="skills">

  <p class="eyebrow">engineering skills developed</p>

  <div class="engineering-list">

    <div>
      <span>01</span>
      <p>
        <strong>Embedded C++</strong> — developing an interactive
        application for a microcontroller.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>02</span>
      <p>
        <strong>Object-oriented design</strong> — using classes and
        inheritance to organize reusable game entities.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>03</span>
      <p>
        <strong>Graphics programming</strong> — representing sprites
        as pixel data and rendering them to an embedded display.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>04</span>
      <p>
        <strong>State management</strong> — coordinating movement,
        interactions, scoring, and graphics inside a continuous loop.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <span>05</span>
      <p>
        <strong>Hardware/software integration</strong> — connecting
        application behavior to a physical microcontroller and display.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

</ProjectCaseLayout>


<style>

  section {
    scroll-margin-top: 110px;

    padding: 0 0 52px;
    margin: 0 0 52px;

    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  section:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  section > .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  section p {
    max-width: 740px;

    color: #3d3e39;

    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .big-copy {
    margin-top: 0;

    color: var(--text);

    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.2vw, 1.8rem);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  h2 {
    max-width: 760px;

    margin: 0 0 24px;

    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 3.2rem);
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;

    font-weight: 580;
  }


  /* ARCHITECTURE */

  .architecture-flow {
  display: grid;

  grid-template-columns:
    minmax(0, 1fr)
    auto
    minmax(0, 1fr)
    auto
    minmax(0, 1fr)
    auto
    minmax(0, 1fr);

  align-items: stretch;

  gap: 12px;

  margin-top: 30px;
}

.architecture-flow > div {
  padding: 18px;

  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
}

.architecture-flow > span {
  align-self: center;

  color: var(--muted);
}

.architecture-flow div > span {
  display: block;

  margin-bottom: 20px;

  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 0.72rem;
}

.architecture-flow strong {
  display: block;

  margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.architecture-flow p {
  margin: 0;

  font-size: 0.88rem;
}

/* CHALLENGES */

.challenge-list {
  margin-top: 30px;

  border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
}

.challenge-list > div {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 48px 1fr;

  gap: 10px;

  padding: 22px 0;

  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}

.challenge-list > div > span {
  color: var(--accent);

  font-size: 0.72rem;
}

.challenge-list strong {
  display: block;

  margin-bottom: 8px;

  font-size: 1.05rem;
}

.challenge-list p {
  margin: 0 0 8px;

  font-size: 0.94rem;
}

.challenge-list .solution {
  margin-bottom: 0;

  color: var(--muted);
}

  /* CODE */

  .code-block {
    margin: 30px 0 10px;

    overflow: hidden;

    background: #11120f;
    border: 1px solid #11120f;
  }

  .code-head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;

    padding: 10px 16px;

    border-bottom: 1px solid #353630;

    color: #a8a9a3;

    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: lowercase;
  }

  .code-block pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 22px;

    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .code-block code {
    color: #f2f1ed;

    font-family:
      "SFMono-Regular",
      Consolas,
      "Liberation Mono",
      monospace;

    font-size: 0.84rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .code-note {
    margin: 0 0 30px;

    color: var(--muted);

    font-size: 0.78rem;

    font-style: italic;
  }


  /* IMPLEMENTATION SKILLS */

  .skill-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;

    gap: 10px;
  }

  .skill-grid > div {
    padding: 18px;

    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .skill-grid .num {
    display: block;

    margin-bottom: 20px;

    color: var(--accent);

    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .skill-grid strong {
    display: block;

    margin-bottom: 7px;
  }

  .skill-grid p {
    margin: 0;

    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }


  /* HARDWARE */

  .hardware-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);

    gap: 10px;

    margin: 28px 0;
  }

  .hardware-grid > div {
    padding: 18px;

    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .hardware-grid span {
    display: block;

    margin-bottom: 18px;

    color: var(--muted);

    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: lowercase;
  }


  /* ENGINEERING SKILLS */

  .engineering-list {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .engineering-list > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 46px 1fr;

    padding: 15px 0;

    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .engineering-list span {
    color: var(--muted);

    font-size: 0.72rem;
  }

  .engineering-list p {
    margin: 0;

    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }


  @media (max-width: 760px) {

    .architecture-flow {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .architecture-flow > span {
      display: none;
    }

    .skill-grid,
    .hardware-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

  }

</style>